<speak>
<p>What if you opened the door of the International Space Station<break time="400ms"/> and stepped outside?</p>
<break time="700ms"/>
<p>Good news first.<break time="300ms"/> <emphasis level="moderate">You can't.</emphasis><break time="400ms"/>
The hatches open inward, and the air inside pushes on them with almost fifteen pounds on every square inch.<break time="300ms"/>
On a hatch this size, that's over <emphasis level="moderate">thirty-five thousand pounds</emphasis>.<break time="300ms"/>
Almost three elephants, holding the door shut.</p>
<break time="700ms"/>
<p>But let's say you're in the airlock<break time="500ms"/> and the outer hatch fails.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p><prosody rate="90%">Whoosh.</prosody><break time="300ms"/> The air rushes out. Anything not tied down goes with it.<break time="300ms"/>
The temperature drops so fast, the cabin fills with fog.<break time="300ms"/>
And then<break time="500ms"/> the sound just fades away.<break time="500ms"/> <prosody rate="85%" volume="soft">Silence.</prosody></p>
<break time="800ms"/>
<p>Now, the most important thing you can do?<break time="300ms"/> <emphasis level="strong">Breathe out.</emphasis><break time="300ms"/>
Hold your breath, and your lungs can rupture.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>You don't explode.<break time="200ms"/> Your blood doesn't boil.<break time="300ms"/> But the water on your tongue does.<break time="400ms"/>
In nineteen sixty-six, a NASA engineer was exposed to near-vacuum in a test chamber.<break time="300ms"/>
The last thing he remembered?<break time="400ms"/> His saliva bubbling on his tongue.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>Your skin swells.<break time="300ms"/> And you have about <emphasis level="moderate">fifteen seconds</emphasis> before you pass out.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>You won't freeze instantly, either.<break time="200ms"/> Space is like a giant thermos.<break time="300ms"/>
In the sun, surfaces hit two hundred fifty degrees.<break time="300ms"/> In the shade,<break time="200ms"/> minus two hundred fifty.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>If someone gets air back to you within about a minute and a half,<break time="200ms"/> you could survive.</p>
<break time="700ms"/>
<p>And here's the wild part.<break time="300ms"/> The station does spring leaks.<break time="300ms"/>
In twenty twenty, the crew tracked one down<break time="500ms"/> using floating tea leaves.</p>
<break time="800ms"/>
<p>So yeah.<break time="400ms"/> <prosody rate="92%">Maybe keep that door closed.</prosody></p>
</speak>
